import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.C3idr2hZ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Continue a local session in ORC","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/session-handoff.md","filePath":"docs/session-handoff.md","lastUpdated":1794954094000}'),a={name:"docs/session-handoff.md"};function i(d,e,r,c,h,l){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="continue-a-local-session-in-orc" tabindex="-1">Continue a local session in ORC <a class="header-anchor" href="#continue-a-local-session-in-orc" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Continue a local session in ORC&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Use the bundled <code>orc-platform</code> skill&#39;s <code>references/execution.md</code> handoff section with Claude Code or Codex. Within Git authorization, the agent commits and pushes the intended work, verifies the remote SHA, calls <code>session_handoff</code>, and uploads its native conversation. The chat session list shows the imported session as <strong>Ready to resume</strong>. Click it to continue in chat, or copy its resume command from the session viewer. The dashboard&#39;s <a href="#send-from-the-dashboard">Send to host</a> move also supports OpenCode and Pi.</p><p>The destination project must already exist in ORC with its folder scope pointing to a repository on the selected execution host. Its configured remote must match the reported repository. ORC does not clone arbitrary repository URLs supplied in an upload. SSH mode installs files on the same SSH host/user that runs the resumed agent, including a Docker container reached through an SSH alias. Persist the agent home and repository volumes if sessions must survive container replacement. The host needs Git, the relevant agent CLI and, for remote file operations, Python 3.</p><h2 id="send-from-the-dashboard" tabindex="-1">Send from the dashboard <a class="header-anchor" href="#send-from-the-dashboard" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Send from the dashboard&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A session can also be moved by a person, without the agent running the sequence: <strong>Send to host…</strong> in the session&#39;s actions menu (the send icon) opens the saved ORC servers from <a href="./desktop-and-web#settings">Settings → Hosts</a>, except the one you are connected to. Pick a host, then one of its projects, optionally leave a note, and send.</p><p>The source installation then runs the same contract on your behalf:</p><ol><li>It snapshots the session&#39;s repository through a temporary Git index — committed, uncommitted and untracked work, without touching the checkout — and leaves secret-looking files (<code>.env*</code>, keys, certificates, tfstate, …) on the source machine.</li><li>It pushes the snapshot as <code>orc/send/&lt;send-id&gt;</code> to the repository&#39;s <code>origin</code> and uploads the native transcript (Claude sidecars included) to the destination.</li><li>The destination prepares its worktree and shows the session as <strong>Ready to resume</strong>; the source row keeps a <strong>Sent to …</strong> badge, and the send result links straight to the destination session. Reopening Send to host on a moved session shows that link again.</li></ol><p>The first chat turn on the destination carries a one-time handoff briefing: where the session came from, the branch and snapshot, which files stayed local, and your note — so the agent knows it resumed on another machine and checks dependencies before relying on them. The destination host&#39;s token and proxy headers travel with the send request only; the source never stores them.</p><p>Sending supports Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Pi sessions on the source server&#39;s own machine, and both installations must reach the same Git remote. The destination project&#39;s checkout must already exist there. OpenCode sessions move between the two machines&#39; OpenCode databases (the destination must have run OpenCode once; legacy pre-2.2 sessions are refused with a hint to open them in OpenCode first). Pi resume in chat needs an enabled Pi provider on the destination, like any Pi conversation. Errors (a missing project, an unreachable host, a diverged remote) are recorded on the send and shown in the dialog; nothing is cleaned up on the destination automatically, so a failed send can be inspected there and retried.</p><h2 id="http-contract" tabindex="-1">HTTP contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#http-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;HTTP contract&quot;">​</a></h2><p>All operations are available through the API. MCP exposes one convenience tool: <code>session_handoff</code> forwards the same initiation request over HTTP. The SDK exposes <code>client.handoffs.create</code>, <code>get</code> and <code>prepare</code>, plus <code>client.sessions.sendPreview</code>, <code>sendTo</code> and <code>sends</code> for the dashboard-driven move.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Endpoint</th><th>Authentication</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Initiate</td><td><code>POST /api/session-handoffs</code></td><td>Bearer token when an API secret is configured; otherwise open</td></tr><tr><td>Upload</td><td><code>PUT /api/session-handoffs/{id}/upload?token=…</code></td><td>Temporary upload capability</td></tr><tr><td>Inspect</td><td><code>GET /api/session-handoffs/{id}</code></td><td>Bearer token when an API secret is configured; otherwise open</td></tr><tr><td>Retry preparation</td><td><code>POST /api/session-handoffs/{id}/prepare</code></td><td>Bearer token when an API secret is configured; otherwise open</td></tr><tr><td>Resume</td><td><code>POST /api/terminals</code> with <code>live_session_id</code></td><td>Existing terminal authentication and workspace rules</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Initiation accepts <code>project</code> (readable name), <code>agent</code> (<code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>opencode</code> or <code>pi</code>), native <code>session_id</code> (UUID for Claude/Codex/Pi, <code>ses_…</code> for OpenCode), <code>source_cwd</code>, <code>repository</code> (credential-free URL), <code>remote</code> (default <code>origin</code>), <code>branch</code>, exact pushed <code>commit</code> (40-character SHA), optional <code>subdirectory</code> (relative repo folder), optional <code>title</code> and optional <code>briefing</code> (delivered with the first resumed chat turn).</p><p>The returned upload link expires after 15 minutes and is single-use. It authorizes only that handoff, not inspection, terminal launch or other API calls. Upload a JSON body:</p><div class="language-json vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">json</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">{</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;version&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;files&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:[{</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;path&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;session.jsonl&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;content&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;native JSONL text</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\\n</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}]}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The full encoded body is limited to 64 MiB and 128 files, including actual streamed bytes regardless of Content-Length. Claude also accepts <code>subagents/agent-*.jsonl</code>. Codex and Pi accept one native log; OpenCode accepts one raw database export (<code>session.jsonl</code> with a <code>session</code> header row and <code>session_message</code> rows). Arbitrary paths, credentials/config and agent SQLite databases are rejected. Native conversation IDs are verified before consuming the link. The skill includes a Python upload helper for Claude/Codex; no SCP or access to the destination filesystem is required on the source machine.</p><h2 id="destination-state" tabindex="-1">Destination state <a class="header-anchor" href="#destination-state" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Destination state&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The upload is stored privately on the API machine. Preparation captures the selected execution host, fetches the configured remote branch and requires its tip to equal the reported SHA. A changed branch requires a new handoff. ORC creates:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>&lt;destination-repo&gt;/.orc/worktrees/&lt;handoff-id&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The local branch is <code>orc/handoff/&lt;handoff-id&gt;</code>, tracking the reported remote branch. Git&#39;s local exclusions keep this directory out of the original checkout&#39;s status. ORC preserves existing branches, checkouts and session files. Conversation history is copied into a new native session id (a UUID for Claude/Codex/Pi, a <code>ses_…</code> id for OpenCode), with structured cwd metadata relocated; historical message/tool text is retained. Custom native storage uses the execution host&#39;s <code>CODEX_HOME</code> or <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code> when set; Pi honors <code>PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR</code>, and OpenCode installs into its database under the execution host&#39;s home.</p><p>Before the first resume, the terminal API verifies the commit, a clean worktree and the native transcript&#39;s presence. Successful launch marks the handoff <code>resumed</code>; later resumes allow the session&#39;s subsequent edits and commits. Existing running terminals are reused by the standard terminal API. Deleting the ORC project keeps its handoff history and session as unassigned; it does not remove the native files or worktree.</p><h2 id="failures-and-limits" tabindex="-1">Failures and limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#failures-and-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Failures and limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Inspect <code>status</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>cwd</code> and <code>live_session_id</code>. Upload returns the handoff record; <code>status: error</code> means bytes were received but preparation failed. Fix the cause and call the prepare endpoint, without uploading again. Preparation records and bundles survive API restarts. A preparation lease can be recovered after five minutes; a ready handoff is returned unchanged on retry. A consumed or expired link cannot upload again.</p><p>Only Git-tracked state is transported. Ignored files, dependencies, environment variables, processes, external attachments, submodule checkouts and LFS hydration need separate preparation. Destination CLI credentials and configuration remain its own. Standalone Codex child-agent sessions are rejected: hand off their parent instead. Active child-agent processes are not migrated. Keep the source idle during the final snapshot and continue work in the destination once it is ready.</p>',24)])])}const f=t(a,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
